tasks.acceptance_prose:
GOAL: a small, PURE module that turns a worker's loosely-declared cost basis into a comparable notion of expense. It is the single place the assumption "when no provider reports a price, a bigger model is more expensive" lives.

WHY IT EXISTS: agentflow now records real `tokens` per attempt (round 9), but `cost.total` in every provider's JSON transcript has been 0, so the cost-reporting and routing code has no idea whether one worker is 100x more expensive than another. The fix is to let an operator DECLARE a basis per worker and to make that declaration comparable. Do NOT try to infer a model's size from its name ("flash", "mini", "pro") - that is magic that would silently change dispatch when a provider renames a model.

NEW FILE src/cost.rs. It must be pure: no I/O, no env, no config access, no new dependencies. Exact public API (other tasks call these - the signatures are a contract):

    /// How a worker's expense is measured, when it declares a basis.
    #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]
    pub enum Basis {
        /// Real money: US dollars per million tokens.
        Priced(f64),
        /// A size proxy: the model's parameter count, in billions.
        Sized(f64),
    }

    /// The declared basis of a worker. A declared PRICE beats a SIZE proxy
    /// (real money is not a proxy). None when neither is declared.
    pub fn basis(params_b: Option<f64>, price_per_mtok_usd: Option<f64>) -> Option<Basis>

    /// Order two workers by expense: Some(Less) when `a` is the CHEAPER.
    /// None when they are NOT comparable, which happens when (i) either side
    /// has no basis, or (ii) the two are different VARIANTS - a $/Mtok price
    /// and a parameter count are incommensurable and must never be converted
    /// into one another.
    pub fn compare(a: Option<Basis>, b: Option<Basis>) -> Option<std::cmp::Ordering>

    /// Dollars for `tokens` at a price in USD per million tokens.
    pub fn estimate_usd(tokens: u64, price_per_mtok_usd: f64) -> f64

    /// A Sized worker's expense RATE relative to the CHEAPEST declared Sized
    /// basis in `all_declared` (the cheapest is exactly 1.0). None when
    /// `all_declared` holds no Sized basis - never invent a reference.
    pub fn size_ratio(params_b: f64, all_declared: &[Basis]) -> Option<f64>

    /// True when a declared number is usable: finite and strictly positive.
    /// This is the single predicate load-time validation uses.
    pub fn is_usable(value: f64) -> bool

`compare` must use `partial_cmp`, so a NaN can never compare as cheaper. Document the "no invented conversion rate" rule in the module doc comment - it is the whole reason `Basis` is an enum instead of one f64 weight.

WIRE IT UP: add `pub mod cost;` to src/lib.rs. Leave every existing re-export exactly as it is (other code depends on them).

TESTS - new file tests/cost_model.rs, each test's name written literally in the file:
1. `fn cost_basis_prefers_a_declared_price_over_a_parameter_count()` - both declared -> Priced; only params -> Sized; only price -> Priced; neither -> None.
2. `fn incommensurable_bases_are_never_compared()` - Priced vs Sized is None (in BOTH directions), Priced vs Priced and Sized vs Sized order correctly (cheaper is Less), and a NaN basis never wins a comparison.
3. `fn a_size_ratio_is_measured_against_the_cheapest_declared_worker()` - 400B against [400B, 32B, Priced(1.0)] is 12.5, the cheapest is 1.0, and a list with no Sized basis yields None.
4. `fn estimate_usd_and_usability_are_exact()` - 1_000_000 tokens at 0.60 is 0.60; is_usable rejects 0, -1, NaN and infinity and accepts 0.5.

Use the `// spec: ...` marker only if you touch a spec - this task does NOT change user-visible behaviour, so it needs no spec change and no openspec command.
DO NOT TOUCH: src/config.rs, src/run.rs, src/router.rs, src/execute.rs, README.md - other tasks in this campaign own them. If you believe a change is needed there, note it in your summary instead of editing.
